{"name":"Eon","hex":"#d4d3c9","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"eon","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Eternity, the duration of the universe.","An immeasurably or indefinitely long period of time."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/eon"}],"entries":[{"title":"Aeon","blocks":[{"text":"The word aeon, also spelled eon, originally meant \"life\", \"vital force\" or \"being\", \"generation\" or \"a period of time\", though it tended to be translated as \"age\" in the sense of \"ages\", \"forever\", \"timeless\" or \"for eternity\". In Greek, it literally refers to the timespan of one hundred years. A cognate Latin word aevum for \"age\" is present in words such as eternal, longevity and mediaeval."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aeon"},{"title":"Geologic time scale","blocks":[{"text":"The geologic time scale or geological time scale describes how geologic time is divided into standardised intervals. It uses the rock record together with the principles of chronostratigraphy to place rock sequences into their relative age positions, and geochronology techniques, such as radiometric dating, to precisely date the boundaries between them. It is used primarily by Earth scientists to describe the timing and relationships of events in geologic history. The time scale has been developed through the study of rock layers and the observation of their relationships and identifying features such as lithologies, paleomagnetic properties, and fossils. The definition of standardised international units of geological time is the responsibility of the International Commission on Stratigraphy (ICS), a constituent body of the International Union of Geological Sciences (IUGS), whose primary objective is to precisely define global chronostratigraphic units of the International Chronostratigraphic Chart (ICC) that are used to define divisions of geological time. The chronostratigraphic divisions are in turn used to define geochronologic units."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fb/Geologic_time_scale_-_spiral_-_ICS_colours_%28light%29_-_path_text.svg/330px-Geologic_time_scale_-_spiral_-_ICS_colours_%28light%29_-_path_text.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Geologic_time_scale"},{"title":"Eon (novel)","blocks":[{"text":"Eon is a science fiction novel by American author Greg Bear published by Bluejay Books in 1985. Eon was nominated for an Arthur C. Clarke Award in 1987. It is the first novel in The Way series; followed by Eternity."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/b/bd/Eon_%28novel%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eon_(novel)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/15/Sketch_of_the_Succession_pf_Strata_and_their_relative_Altitudes.jpg/500px-Sketch_of_the_Succession_pf_Strata_and_their_relative_Altitudes.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/15/Sketch_of_the_Succession_pf_Strata_and_their_relative_Altitudes.jpg/1280px-Sketch_of_the_Succession_pf_Strata_and_their_relative_Altitudes.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/15/Sketch_of_the_Succession_pf_Strata_and_their_relative_Altitudes.jpg/500px-Sketch_of_the_Succession_pf_Strata_and_their_relative_Altitudes.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Sketch of the Succession of Strata and their Relative Altitudes (William Smith)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASketch_of_the_Succession_pf_Strata_and_their_relative_Altitudes.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ed/Pierre_Lecomte_du_No%C3%BCy_-_LES_AGES_DE_LA_VIE_SUR_LA_TERRE_-_in_L%27Homme_et_sa_destin%C3%A9e_-_1947.jpg/500px-Pierre_Lecomte_du_No%C3%BCy_-_LES_AGES_DE_LA_VIE_SUR_LA_TERRE_-_in_L%27Homme_et_sa_destin%C3%A9e_-_1947.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ed/Pierre_Lecomte_du_No%C3%BCy_-_LES_AGES_DE_LA_VIE_SUR_LA_TERRE_-_in_L%27Homme_et_sa_destin%C3%A9e_-_1947.jpg/1280px-Pierre_Lecomte_du_No%C3%BCy_-_LES_AGES_DE_LA_VIE_SUR_LA_TERRE_-_in_L%27Homme_et_sa_destin%C3%A9e_-_1947.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ed/Pierre_Lecomte_du_No%C3%BCy_-_LES_AGES_DE_LA_VIE_SUR_LA_TERRE_-_in_L%27Homme_et_sa_destin%C3%A9e_-_1947.jpg/500px-Pierre_Lecomte_du_No%C3%BCy_-_LES_AGES_DE_LA_VIE_SUR_LA_TERRE_-_in_L%27Homme_et_sa_destin%C3%A9e_-_1947.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"One example of an obsolete geological time scale (France, mid-1940s).","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APierre_Lecomte_du_No%C3%BCy_-_LES_AGES_DE_LA_VIE_SUR_LA_TERRE_-_in_L'Homme_et_sa_destin%C3%A9e_-_1947.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/54/MIT_12.007_Geobiology%2C_Spring_2007_-_Graph_of_the_Geological_Timescale.jpg/500px-MIT_12.007_Geobiology%2C_Spring_2007_-_Graph_of_the_Geological_Timescale.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/54/MIT_12.007_Geobiology%2C_Spring_2007_-_Graph_of_the_Geological_Timescale.jpg/1280px-MIT_12.007_Geobiology%2C_Spring_2007_-_Graph_of_the_Geological_Timescale.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/54/MIT_12.007_Geobiology%2C_Spring_2007_-_Graph_of_the_Geological_Timescale.jpg/500px-MIT_12.007_Geobiology%2C_Spring_2007_-_Graph_of_the_Geological_Timescale.jpg?utm